Subject: [PATCH 10336/11362] Lower the supervisor's default window so it can
 actually fire (REQ-ARC-WMTE-6780)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The default was 400, which IS the action budget. A stagnation window of 400
actions essentially cannot complete before a run ends, so the supervisor could
not fire, and the cross-run refinement tool (REQ-ARC-WMTE-6720, floor of 10
firings per arm) sat at 2 and reported INSUFFICIENT EVIDENCE. That was an honest
answer to a question the setting had made unanswerable, and it would have stayed
that way forever.

MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. From
docs/research-notes/arc-induction-round-records-fine-read-2026-08-27.md:

  supon      window 400, applied -> 0 firings
  supshadow  window 400, shadow  -> 0 firings
  supwindow  window 120, applied -> 2 redirects per cell

So 120 is a value with evidence behind it, not a guess that felt smaller.

WHY THIS IS SAFE, verified in the source before changing anything. The call site
reads:

    redirect = supervisor.observe(snapshot)
    if redirect is not None and self._trajectory_supervisor_applies:
        self._apply_trajectory_redirect(redirect)

`observe()` runs UNCONDITIONALLY; only application is gated. Shadow is the default
(REQ-ARC-WMTE-6660). So this changes what every run RECORDS and changes scored
behaviour only for a run that sets CARNOT_ARC_TRAJECTORY_SUPERVISOR=1. A run that
does set it now gets redirects sooner — intended, and the reason the value is
measured rather than picked.

That safety argument is pinned by a test: if the applied flag ever becomes the
default, the window change stops being free and the suite says so.

This is item 1 of the three queued in ops/known-issues.md 2026-08-30, and the
cheapest — no GPU, and it fills the ledger the other two will be judged against.

38 tests; 3/3 mutations RED (default back to 400, env override ignored, applied
becoming the default). Restores byte-identical.

Noted while reading the evidence: the supwindow run was itself truncated by
`exit=143` — SIGTERM — making it the THIRD unexplained external kill of a
long-running measurement in this record. REQ-INFRA-6830 now leaves a receipt for
the next one.

